using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using RecursiveAI.OneHtml.Css;
using Xunit;

namespace RecursiveAI.OneHtml.Tests.Style;

/// <summary>
/// The user-agent style of a range input's parts and how author rules and state reach them. The parts are real elements in Chrome's shadow tree, so
/// <c>getComputedStyle</c> cannot name them; every expectation is the computed style the DevTools protocol (<c>CSS.getComputedStyleForNode</c>) returned
/// for the track and thumb nodes of Chrome for Testing 156.0.8078.47, after the same stylesheet, attributes and forced pseudo-classes. Values are written as the
/// library prints them (<c>transparent</c> is Chrome's <c>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)</c>, <c>0</c> is <c>0px</c>). Sizes, positions and other used values from layout are
/// not compared, nor are the properties where the library's input style differs from Chrome's own (the input's default font, cursor and colour, which the parts inherit).
/// Chrome has no fill part, so the fill is not compared. A forced pseudo-class is the matching <see cref="StyleContext"/>.
/// </summary>
